@test

// `core:time` had **no test file at all** until this one, which is how a
// package whose entire job is two clocks and a wait shipped with nothing
// pinning either clock or the wait. It is written now because the package was
// rewritten — `sysclock.c` and `sysclock.h` are gone and the `clock_gettime` /
// `nanosleep` calls, the millisecond arithmetic and the wall-clock fallback are
// Dado — and a rewrite whose old behaviour nothing recorded is a rewrite nobody
// can check.
//
// **What is actually claimed here**, since reading a clock twice is not a test
// of this package:
//
//   * **`now` moves forwards and never backwards.** That is the one promise the
//     package makes about it, and it is the promise the restatement of `struct
//     timespec` could silently break — a `tv_nsec` read at the wrong width or
//     the wrong offset gives readings that jump around.
//   * **`now` and `unix` are different clocks.** `now` has an unspecified
//     origin and `unix` is seconds since 1970, so they are orders of magnitude
//     apart; a rewrite that accidentally wired `now` to `time()` would pass
//     every monotonicity check and fail this one.
//   * **`nap` actually waits, and `since` sees the wait.** This is the only
//     check that crosses all three of `nanosleep`, the `tv_sec`/`tv_nsec` split
//     and `now`, so a millisecond-to-nanosecond factor that is off by a power
//     of ten shows up here and nowhere else.
//   * **`nap` returns immediately for a non-positive wait** rather than
//     handing `nanosleep` a negative `tv_nsec`, which is EINVAL and which the
//     old `.c` guarded against by hand.
//   * **`ms_since` agrees with `since`.** Two views of one reading; they are
//     separate code and can drift.
//   * **`ms_since` holds an uptime past 24.85 days.** It answered `i32` once,
//     which runs out at 2^31 ms; the arithmetic is checked through
//     `ms_between` with both readings supplied, since no test waits a month.
//   * **`Duration`'s constructors and readers agree**, and its operators keep
//     the tag; **`Instant` never goes backwards** over many readings and is
//     the clock `now` reads; **`sleep` waits at least its whole duration**,
//     with no floor below the request, because it is the one wait that
//     promises; and **`unix_nanos` is `unix` in nanoseconds**. These run on
//     every platform, and on Windows they are the claims its arm is held to.
//
// **The bounds are loose on purpose, and only in the direction that can lie.**
// A sleep may overrun by any amount — this runs under sanitizers on a shared
// two-core box — so nothing here has an upper bound tight enough to fail on a
// slow machine. The lower bounds are the real claims: a `nap(50)` that returns
// in 1 ms did not sleep, and no amount of scheduling noise produces that.
package time

i32 test():
    bool ok = true

    // ── now moves forwards ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Ten readings rather than two: a single pair can pass on a clock that is
    // simply garbage but happens to come out ordered once.
    f64 previous = now()
    for i in i64(0)..<10:
        f64 current = now()
        ok = #expect(current >= previous, "now() went backwards: ", previous, " then ", current) && ok
        previous = current

    // ── now and unix are not the same clock ──────────────────────────────────
    //
    // `unix` is seconds since 1970 and is therefore past 1.7e9 on any machine
    // whose clock is set at all; `now`'s origin is unspecified but is boot or
    // process start on every platform this builds for, so it is small. The
    // claim is the *separation*, which is what the package's whole split is.
    i64 wall = unix()
    ok = #expect(wall > 1700000000, "unix() is not a plausible wall clock: ", wall) && ok
    f64 mono = now()
    ok = #expect(mono < 1000000000.0, "now() looks like wall time, not a monotonic clock: ", mono) && ok

    // ── nap waits, and since sees it ─────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // 50 ms, with a floor of 40: the floor is below the request because a
    // clock's resolution and an early wake from a signal are both allowed, and
    // there is deliberately no ceiling.
    f64 before = now()
    nap(50)
    f64 slept = since(before)
    ok = #expect(slept >= 0.040, "nap(50) returned after ", slept, "s, so it did not sleep") && ok

    // The same interval through the other accessor. `ms_since` is separate
    // arithmetic over the same reading and can drift from `since` on its own.
    i64 slept_ms = ms_since(before)
    ok = #expect(slept_ms >= 40, "ms_since() reported ", slept_ms, "ms over a 50ms nap") && ok

    // ── ms_since past 24.85 days ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // The uptime an `i32` of milliseconds cannot hold, through the arithmetic
    // `ms_since` is, with both readings supplied. Started one second after a
    // boot 30 days ago, read now: 2,591,999,000 ms, which is past 2^31. The
    // `i32` this used to be answered `-2147483648` here, then `2147483647`.
    f64 day = 86400.0
    i64 month = ms_between(1.0, 30.0 * day)
    ok = #expect(month == 2591999000, "ms_between over 30 days gave ", month) && ok
    ok = #expect(ms_between(0.0, 2147484.0) == 2147484000, "a second past the i32's end did not come back whole") && ok
    // A reading from the future is a negative interval, not a wrapped one.
    ok = #expect(ms_between(10.0, 9.5) == 0 - 500, "a backwards interval gave ", ms_between(10.0, 9.5)) && ok
    // A clock that returned nonsense saturates rather than trapping: NaN is 0.
    f64 zero = 0.0
    f64 nan = zero / zero
    ok = #expect(ms_between(nan, 5.0) == 0, "a NaN reading gave ", ms_between(nan, 5.0)) && ok

    // ── a non-positive nap returns, and returns at once ──────────────────────
    //
    // Not merely "does not crash": a negative millisecond count that reached
    // `nanosleep` would be EINVAL and an instant return, which is
    // indistinguishable from the guard working. What is checked is that both
    // calls are cheap *and* that the clock is still sane afterwards.
    f64 guard_start = now()
    nap(0)
    nap(0 - 5)
    f64 guard_elapsed = since(guard_start)
    ok = #expect(guard_elapsed >= 0.0, "a non-positive nap moved the clock backwards") && ok
    ok = #expect(guard_elapsed < 1.0, "nap(0) and nap(-5) took ", guard_elapsed, "s, so they slept") && ok

    // ── since is measured from now, not from zero ────────────────────────────
    //
    // `since(now())` is the degenerate case and the one an off-by-an-origin
    // bug produces a huge number for.
    f64 immediate = since(now())
    ok = #expect(immediate >= 0.0, "since(now()) was negative: ", immediate) && ok
    ok = #expect(immediate < 1.0, "since(now()) was ", immediate, "s, so it is not measured from now") && ok

    // ── Duration: the constructors agree with each other ─────────────────────
    //
    // Four constructors and five readers over one integer; a factor off by a
    // power of ten in any of them shows up as a disagreement here.
    ok = #expect(seconds(2) == milliseconds(2000), "seconds(2) != milliseconds(2000)") && ok
    ok = #expect(milliseconds(3) == microseconds(3000), "milliseconds(3) != microseconds(3000)") && ok
    ok = #expect(microseconds(5) == nanoseconds(5000), "microseconds(5) != nanoseconds(5000)") && ok
    ok = #expect(as_nanoseconds(seconds(1)) == 1000000000, "a second is not 1e9 ns") && ok
    ok = #expect(as_microseconds(milliseconds(7)) == 7000, "7 ms is not 7000 us") && ok
    ok = #expect(as_milliseconds(nanoseconds(2999999)) == 2, "2.999999 ms did not truncate to 2") && ok
    ok = #expect(as_seconds(milliseconds(1500)) == 1, "1500 ms did not truncate to 1 s") && ok
    ok = #expect(as_seconds_f64(milliseconds(1500)) == 1.5, "1500 ms is not 1.5 s") && ok
    // The operators come with the tag, and a backwards interval is negative.
    Duration sum = milliseconds(250) + milliseconds(750)
    ok = #expect(sum == seconds(1), "250 ms + 750 ms is not a second") && ok
    ok = #expect(as_milliseconds(milliseconds(250) - milliseconds(750)) == 0 - 500, "a negative Duration did not stay negative") && ok
    // An uptime no `i32` holds, which is where the old millisecond count broke.
    ok = #expect(as_milliseconds(seconds(30 * 86400)) == 2592000000, "30 days did not survive as milliseconds") && ok

    // ── Instant never goes backwards ─────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // A thousand readings: the claim is the monotonic clock's one promise, and
    // on Windows it is the counter divided by its frequency in two halves, an
    // arithmetic that would jump at a whole-second boundary if it were wrong.
    Instant last = instant()
    for i in i64(0)..<1000:
        Instant next = instant()
        ok = #expect(i64(between(last, next)) >= 0, "instant() went backwards by ", i64(between(next, last)), " ns") && ok
        last = next

    // `between` and `add` are inverses, and `elapsed` is measured from now.
    Instant base = instant()
    Instant later = add(base, milliseconds(40))
    ok = #expect(between(base, later) == milliseconds(40), "add then between lost the interval") && ok
    ok = #expect(between(later, base) == nanoseconds(0) - milliseconds(40), "a reversed between was not negative") && ok
    Duration fresh = elapsed(instant())
    ok = #expect(i64(fresh) >= 0 && fresh < seconds(1), "elapsed(instant()) was ", i64(fresh), " ns") && ok

    // The same clock under both surfaces: `now` and `instant` agree to well
    // within a second, so the f64 surface is not on some other clock.
    f64 drift = f64(i64(instant())) / 1000000000.0 - now()
    ok = #expect(drift > -1.0 && drift < 1.0, "now() and instant() disagree by ", drift, "s") && ok

    // ── sleep sleeps at least its duration ───────────────────────────────────
    //
    // **No floor below the request this time**, unlike `nap`'s: `sleep`
    // promises the whole interval on the same clock this reads, so a single
    // nanosecond short is a failure. Odd numbers so a whole-millisecond
    // rounding is visible.
    Instant s0 = instant()
    sleep(milliseconds(33))
    Duration slept_for = elapsed(s0)
    ok = #expect(slept_for >= milliseconds(33), "sleep(33ms) returned after ", i64(slept_for), " ns") && ok

    Instant s1 = instant()
    sleep(microseconds(1500))
    Duration short_sleep = elapsed(s1)
    ok = #expect(short_sleep >= microseconds(1500), "sleep(1.5ms) returned after ", i64(short_sleep), " ns") && ok

    // Zero and negative return at once.
    Instant s2 = instant()
    sleep(nanoseconds(0))
    sleep(milliseconds(0 - 50))
    Duration no_sleep = elapsed(s2)
    ok = #expect(no_sleep < seconds(1), "sleep(0) and sleep(-50ms) took ", i64(no_sleep), " ns") && ok

    // ── unix_nanos is the wall clock, in nanoseconds ─────────────────────────
    //
    // Past 2023 in nanoseconds, and the same second as `unix()` give or take
    // the one that may tick between the two reads. On Windows this is the
    // 1601 epoch converted, so an epoch constant that was wrong by a day
    // fails the agreement and one wrong by a unit fails the magnitude.
    i64 wall_ns_read = unix_nanos()
    i64 wall_s_read = unix()
    ok = #expect(wall_ns_read > 1700000000000000000, "unix_nanos() is not a plausible wall clock: ", wall_ns_read) && ok
    i64 skew = wall_s_read - wall_ns_read / 1000000000
    ok = #expect(skew >= 0 && skew <= 1, "unix() and unix_nanos() are ", skew, "s apart") && ok

    if ok: return 0
    return 1
